<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#secretary-general-of-the-united-nations">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Secretary-General of the United Nations</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Secretary-General of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> is the chief administrative officer of the United Nations and head of its Secretariat, appointed by the General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council, usually for five-year terms. The office has also become an important diplomatic role in mediating conflicts. Holders have included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dag-hammarskjold">Dag Hammarskjöld</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-thant">U Thant</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-perez-de-cuellar">Javier Pérez de Cuéllar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/secretary-general-of-the-united-nations.jpg" alt="Secretary-General of the United Nations" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Commission (Christophe Licoppe) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ant%C3%B3nio_Guterres,_23.03.23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The role of the secretary-general and of the Secretariat is laid out in Chapter XV, Articles 97 to 101, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a>. The Charter calls the secretary-general the chief administrative officer of the organisation and gives the power to make employment decisions about Secretariat staff. It also empowers the secretary-general to inform the Security Council of any matter which in his opinion may threaten the maintenance of international peace and security. The qualifications, selection process and tenure of the office are open to interpretation and have been established by custom. The secretary-general also chairs the Chief Executives Board for Coordination, which brings together the heads of all UN funds, programmes and specialised agencies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Appointment is by the General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council, so any of its five permanent members can veto a nomination. By precedent the appointee may not be a citizen of a permanent member, and most secretaries-general have been compromise candidates from middle powers with little prior fame. The length of the term is discretionary, but every secretary-general since 1971 has been appointed to a five-year term, and every one since 1961 was chosen for a second term except Boutros Boutros-Ghali, vetoed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1996. Incumbents have avoided seeking a third term since 1981, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> cast sixteen vetoes against a third term for Kurt Waldheim.</p><p>Many powers are informal, and some holders have taken activist roles while others have been more administrative. Much depends on the use of their good offices, steps taken in public and in private to prevent international disputes from arising, escalating or spreading. Examples include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dag-hammarskjold">Dag Hammarskjöld</a>'s promotion of an armistice between the warring parties of the Arab-Israel conflict, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-thant">U Thant</a>'s role in de-escalating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javier-perez-de-cuellar">Javier Pérez de Cuéllar</a>'s negotiation of a ceasefire in the Iran-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq-war">Iraq War</a>. Hammarskjöld is the only holder to have died in office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Observers have variously called the office the world's most visible bully pulpit or the world's moderator. The official residence, a townhouse at 3 Sutton Place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> built for Anne Morgan in 1921, was given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> by Arthur A. Houghton Jr. in 1972; before that, secretaries-general chose where to live.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Secretary-General of the United Nations” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twentieth century Sweden’s welfare model and diplomats such as Raoul Wallenberg and Dag Hammarskjöld, <b>Secretary-General of the United Nations</b> from 1953 to 1961, gave it an international standing out of proportion to its size.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Burmese diplomat U Thant, a close friend of Prime Minister U Nu, served as <b>Secretary-General of the United Nations</b> from 1961 to 1971, the first Asian in that post.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Javier Pérez de Cuéllar served as <b>Secretary-General of the United Nations</b> from 1982 to 1991.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The General Assembly appointed Norway's foreign minister Trygve Lie as the first <b>Secretary-General of the United Nations</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
